      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "I support improving world discovery in VRChat, but I strongly oppose introducing a Dislike button. Instead, I propose a private, Like-only system, with voting data accessible only to the VRChat team.\n\nMy biggest concern is that Dislikes could be weaponized for harassment and retaliation. VRChat worlds are not just content; many are important gathering places for communities. Personal disputes, political disagreements, or hostility toward particular creators could lead to coordinated negative voting. Even trust-based vote weighting cannot fully prevent this, especially when established users participate.\n\nDislikes also provide little constructive feedback. A negative vote does not explain whether a world has performance issues, confusing controls, or simply does not match someone’s preferences. Bug reports and optional private feedback would be much more useful.\n\nFurthermore, ratings could discourage creativity and exploration. Small, experimental, or niche worlds might become harder to discover if ratings influence recommendations. VRChat should preserve its culture of exploring unfamiliar worlds rather than relying on popularity scores.\n\nI am also concerned about iterative development. Projects such as JR West’s Virtual Hiroshima Station have evolved through user feedback after release. If early negative ratings continue to affect a world even after improvements are made, creators may hesitate to release projects before they are fully polished. This could also create barriers for businesses and public organizations considering VRChat.\n\nFor these reasons, I ask VRChat to reconsider the Dislike button and introduce a Like-only system instead. Vote counts and results should remain private, including from world creators, and should be used cautiously alongside Favorites and other signals rather than creating a simple popularity ranking. Constructive feedback and safety reports should be handled separately from subjective ratings.\n\nI appreciate the VRChat team’s efforts to improve world discovery. I simply want to protect the creative diversity, freedom to experiment, and communities that make VRChat special.\n\nPlease reconsider the Dislike button before implementing it.",
      "viewerReactions": null,
      "voteID": null
    }
  ],
  "companyID": "58c62e995865713647a7115d",
